<PromptTokenLimit name="PTL-PromptTokenLimit" continueOnError="false" enabled="true">
  <DisplayName>Prompt Token Limit</DisplayName>
  <!--
    This policy is SpikeArrest for tokens: it throttles the RATE of prompt
    tokens per unit time. It is not a per-request size cap. The required
    element is <Rate>, not <Limit>; <Limit> is not in this policy's schema and
    its absence does not fail bundle validation, it fails at DEPLOY time as a
    NullPointerException in SpikeArrestExecution.getConstantRate, because
    PromptTokenLimit is built on the SpikeArrest machinery. See BUILD-LOG #39.

    KEYED PER DEPARTMENT AND APPLICATION since 2026-09-15 (was department
    only). The department-wide token BUDGET is LTQ-Enforce; this is the
    per-application RATE guard, so one application's burst cannot exhaust the
    other's headroom. The two are complementary: a budget catches slow grinding
    waste a rate never sees, a rate catches bursts a budget only notices after
    the damage.

    RAISED FROM 200000pm, which was a live defect. On 2026-09-15 it rejected a
    real Sales-Agent job:

      429 policies.prompttokenlimit.PromptTokenLimitViolation
      "Allowed rate : MessageRate{capacity=200000, period=Minutes}"
      ReportCompiler failed after 3 attempt(s)

    200000pm sat BELOW a single legitimate call. Sales-Agent's ReportCompiler
    concatenates every domain finding into one prompt, roughly 156,000 tokens
    (601,527 chars of findings plus a 24,749-char catalog). The three retries
    then re-sent that same prompt against an already-empty budget, which is why
    retrying a capacity error makes it worse rather than better.

    20000000pm is sized for measured multi-user load, not for one job:
      Translation   ~124,000 prompt tokens/min per job (measured)  -> ~160 concurrent
      Sales-Agent   ~300,000 prompt tokens/min per job (estimated) -> ~65 concurrent
    A genuine runaway is still caught: a retry loop at the 100ps SpikeArrest
    ceiling with ReportCompiler-sized prompts is ~900,000,000 tokens/min, 45x
    over this limit.

    The value comes from a developer-app attribute so either application can be
    re-tuned WITHOUT redeploying this bundle. The literal is the fallback if the
    attribute is missing or misspelled, mirroring the count/countRef pattern in
    Q-PerUserPerMinute: a known value beats unpredictable behaviour.

    VERIFY THE ref ACTUALLY APPLIES. Set one app's attribute deliberately low
    and confirm that app, and only that app, throttles at the lower value. If
    PromptTokenLimit ignores ref and uses the literal, the per-application
    counters still separate correctly and this will LOOK correct while
    enforcing the wrong number. That is the failure mode most likely to go
    unnoticed here.
  -->
  <Identifier ref="llm_dept_app"/>
  <Rate ref="verifyapikey.VA-ApiKey.developer.app.attribute.llm_prompt_tokens_per_min">20000000pm</Rate>
  <UseEffectiveCount>true</UseEffectiveCount>
</PromptTokenLimit>
